---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CountsTable from "../components/CountsTable.astro";
import ScenarioTable from "../components/ScenarioTable.astro";
import TimingTable from "../components/TimingTable.astro";

// The component tables are rendered FROM the repository's own measurement
// artefacts, not from figures retyped into this file. The counts come from the
// baseline CI gates against on every push, so a number here is a number a
// machine checked. The timing summary is only rendered when a CI run has
// produced one: a microsecond taken on somebody's laptop is not evidence
// anybody else can use, and this page would rather show nothing than show that.
---

<Base
  title="Benchmark — waypoint"
  description="Two lanes: deterministic counts gated in CI, and microseconds from Chrome's own trace that are printed and never gated. The harness publishes its own resolution above its results."
>
  <h1>Benchmark</h1>

  <p class="lead">
    <strong>waypoint is behind on all four
    scenarios, at every size.</strong> The report says so in those words, and
    prints the rows it loses before its own table.
  </p>

  <!-- Four findings, divided by the faint rule rather than sat in four filled
       blocks. The recess (`bg-surface-low`) is not free on this page: every
       table below it uses that ground to mark which rows are ours, so spending
       it here on a summary would leave two different claims wearing one
       ground. `mt-6` stays because nothing in the stylesheet spaces an element
       after `.lead`; 1.5rem is `--gap-block`, the value the sheet would have
       used. -->
  <ol class="mt-6 border-b border-outline-variant">
    <li class="border-t border-outline-variant py-4 text-sm">
      Two of the four losses are won by <strong>not showing the message</strong>:
      one configuration reports only at submit, and one reports nothing at all.
      The other two are beaten by waypoint's own uncontrolled binding.
    </li>
    <li class="border-t border-outline-variant py-4 text-sm">
      On the timing lane a keystroke at 201 fields measures{" "}
      <strong><span class="font-mono measured">0.57×</span> the hand-written
      reference</strong>. The harness <strong>refuses to call it a win</strong>.
      Its calibration ladder resolves nothing that far under the floor.
    </li>
    <li class="border-t border-outline-variant py-4 text-sm">
      <strong>K5 is what coalescing is worth.</strong> Five characters, one
      settle at the end. At 201 fields both waypoint subjects run{" "}
      <strong>1 validator pass, 201 paths judged</strong>; the other four each
      run <strong>5 passes and 1005 paths judged</strong>. The other three
      scenarios drain after every keystroke, which is the one arrangement where
      coalescing can buy nothing.
    </li>
    <li class="border-t border-outline-variant py-4 text-sm">
      Every integer on the counts lane is gated. CI fails when one moves, in
      either direction.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p class="caption">
    The lane asks whether a commit happens, not what one costs.{" "}
    <code>useField</code> subscribes to the value cell, so a keystroke is a
    render; <code>useUncontrolledField</code> subscribes imperatively and writes
    the DOM node, which is what <code>register</code> does for react-hook-form.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== counts -->
  <h2>The counts lane, which is gated</h2>
  <p>These integers do not depend on the machine. That is why they can be gated.</p>

  <CountsTable />

  <p class="caption">
    <code>validatorPasses</code> and <code>paths judged</code> sit beside the
    render counts, so a zero-commit row is never free. The two react-hook-form
    rows commit nothing for a keystroke and still hand the whole 201-path root
    to the schema. The <code>on-submit</code> row does not even do that, by
    documented design.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The fiber column is{" "}
    <strong class="text-on-surface">leaves + a constant</strong>. The constant
    is what differs. At 31, 61 and 201 fields the hand-written reference
    reports 39, 69 and 209: React cloning the sibling list it descended
    through, which every implementation that commits pays. waypoint adds 3, at
    every size.
  </p>

  <ScenarioTable />

  <p class="caption">
    The middle column is where the deferred pass costs rather than saves: with{" "}
    <code>useField</code> the value lands in one task and the verdict in the
    next, so React cannot batch them and commits twice.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The last column is what the whole-root pass was built for. A rule reporting
    against a field nobody touched costs the uncontrolled waypoint row one
    commit and one schema pass. It costs the react-hook-form <code>deps</code>{" "}
    configuration two passes and 402 paths judged.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== time -->
  <h2>The timing lane, which is never gated</h2>

  <TimingTable />

  <p class="caption">
    Headless Chrome over CDP. Every figure comes from the browser's own trace,
    not from page JavaScript. Order alternates every pair, and both members of a
    pair run in one tracing session on two origins, so they are two renderer
    processes.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    Before it compares anything it measures itself: a known cost injected at
    seven sizes, and every subject run against a byte-identical twin of itself.
    A band that overlaps the twin's prints{" "}
    <code class="verdict">indistinguishable</code>, never a win.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== excluded -->
  <p class="caption">
    Each is published with the measurement that excluded it, in{" "}
    <code>docs/measurements-forms-time.md</code> §4.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    Both lanes are run by <code>npm run bench:forms</code> and{" "}
    <code>npm run bench:forms:time</code>, and the design that argues for every
    choice above is in <code>docs/design/form-benchmark.md</code>.
  </p>

  <!-- ================================================== a third lane -->
  <h2>A third lane, at the compiler</h2>
  <p>
    <code>npm run bench:types</code> measures what the path types cost{" "}
    <code>tsc</code>, in the compiler's own accounting: the union built once per
    shape at <span class="font-mono measured">77–137</span> instantiations per
    leaf, an accepted <code>useField</code> at{" "}
    <span class="font-mono measured">275.0</span>, a refused one at{" "}
    <span class="font-mono measured">475,034</span>, and the depth where the
    budget stops.{" "}
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Seven path segments are addressable and
    eight are not.</strong>
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    <strong class="text-on-surface">Printed and never gated.</strong>{" "}
    <code>typescript</code> sits at a caret range, so a patch bump would rewrite
    every figure and the failure would point at <code>npm update</code> rather
    than at the change that made a type worse.
  </p>

  <p class="caption">
    The escape hatch is measured too, rather than asserted.{" "}
    <a
      class="link"
      href="https://github.com/maroonedog/waypoint/blob/master/docs/measurements-types.md"
      >docs/measurements-types.md →</a
    >
  </p>
</Base>
